{"about":"Computed by tools/compute_limits.js from data/settings.json (limits) and the templates drawn by src/figure.js. Do not edit by hand.","status":"estimate","verdict":"Three limits come from the drawn body itself. Widen the average soldier at fixed bones and the arms are forced out first, the thighs close next, and the hands lose the face last. Drawn from their girths, the chart's biggest bodybuilders are already past the first two.","method":"Each template is drawn at 1.80 m and widened at fixed bones: every width and depth of the trunk and limbs times the same factor, while the joints, head, hands and feet stay as they are. At the factor where the limit is reached, the drawn shape is scored at 1,060 kg/m³ and 7.5% fat: the measuring tool reads the head and body from the front and side views, and the arms, which stand far out at these widths, are added as round limbs. The headline is the median soldier's shape; the band runs across six templates, from the 5th-percentile soldier to Ronnie Coleman's shape. Every figure on the chart is drawn and scored the same way, so the page can say which are already past each limit.","notes":["No study gives these limits. Each threshold is an estimate built from sourced pieces: what stops a joint (soft tissue meeting soft tissue), how far ordinary men can move, and how far daily tasks need. The sources are listed under “Where the body stops working” below.","This is flat, front-view geometry on drawn templates. Real soft tissue squashes, arms can sit a little in front of the body, and shoulders shrug, so each limit is a band, not a line.","A softer line comes earlier. Unaided eating and drinking use about 130 to 136° of elbow bend; with elbow motion braced to between 75° and 120°, 49 of 50 adults still did all 12 daily tasks tested, using the joints next to the elbow to compensate (Vasen 1995).","The bands are for human-shaped bodies. Rex and Ollie are not built like the template, and the limits were not tested on their outlines: their thighs touch in the art, but their arms are posed out (an A-pose), so whether they could hang down is untested.","These are shapes scored lean (7.5% fat). A fat body reaches the same outline at a lower score: ordinary obese men's thighs already touch, and very obese people step wider."],"elbow_crease_fraction_of_upper_arm":0.4312,"definitions":{"arms_angle_deg":30,"thighs_fraction":1,"normal_max_flexion_deg":145,"max_flexion_deg":100,"density_kg_m3":1060,"fat_pct":7.5,"height_m":1.8,"arm_max_deg":88},"templates":[{"id":"ansur2_m_p50","name":"US Army men, 50th percentile (ANSUR II)","score_at_f1":25.8,"elbow_flexion_at_f1_deg":145,"arm_rest_angle_at_f1_deg":7.7,"arms":{"f":1.2539,"score":37.87},"thighs":{"f":1.3464,"score":42.89},"face":{"f":2.837,"score":162.66}},{"id":"ansur2_m_p5","name":"US Army men, 5th percentile (ANSUR II)","score_at_f1":22.04,"elbow_flexion_at_f1_deg":147.5,"arm_rest_angle_at_f1_deg":1.9,"arms":{"f":1.3824,"score":38.28},"thighs":{"f":1.483,"score":43.31},"face":{"f":3.0031,"score":153.28}},{"id":"ansur2_m_p95","name":"US Army men, 95th percentile (ANSUR II)","score_at_f1":29.83,"elbow_flexion_at_f1_deg":142.5,"arm_rest_angle_at_f1_deg":15.9,"arms":{"f":1.1434,"score":37.38},"thighs":{"f":1.2382,"score":42.99},"face":{"f":2.6928,"score":172.17}},{"id":"steve_reeves_1947","name":"Steve Reeves (Mr. America 1947)","score_at_f1":25.86,"elbow_flexion_at_f1_deg":141,"arm_rest_angle_at_f1_deg":16.1,"arms":{"f":1.1648,"score":33.64},"thighs":{"f":1.3803,"score":44.9},"face":{"f":2.6098,"score":137.04}},{"id":"ronnie_coleman","name":"Ronnie Coleman (8x Mr. Olympia)","score_at_f1":40.76,"elbow_flexion_at_f1_deg":131.3,"arm_rest_angle_at_f1_deg":39.4,"arms":{"f":0.9014,"score":34.13},"thighs":{"f":0.9711,"score":38.73},"face":{"f":2.1415,"score":149.44}},{"id":"ansur2_m_largest_arm","name":"US Army man with the largest flexed arm (ANSUR II, one person)","score_at_f1":34.51,"elbow_flexion_at_f1_deg":134.8,"arm_rest_angle_at_f1_deg":27.7,"arms